{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.AppendixSurreals.collatz_block_descent","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.collatz_block_descent","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.AppendixSurreals.QCollatz"],"declarations":20,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.chained_oddPreimage_forces_deriv_eq_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.chained_oddPreimage_forces_deriv_eq_two","kind":"theorem","line":413,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L413-L440","doc":"**The chained-odd-preimage criterion, machine-checked.** Differentiating\n`[3]_q·Q = [2]_q·P - 1` at `w` (where `[3]_q` vanishes) gives\n\n    (1 + 2w)·Q(w) = P(w) + (w+1)·P'(w),\n\nwhich is carried here as the hypothesis `hrel` — it is ordinary\ndifferentiation, not formalized in this file. Given `P(w) = -w` (so `P`\nadmits an odd-preimage at all), that preimage satisfies `Q(w) = -w` — the\ncondition for a **second** chained odd-preimage — only if `P'(w) = 2`.\n\nContrapositive, which is how it is used: `P'(w) ≠ 2` rules out a chained\nodd-preimage. This is the criterion the probes evaluate, previously derived\nby hand; the cancellation is of the **unit** `w+1`, so no domain hypothesis\nis needed.\n\n(The converse would need to cancel `1 + 2w`, which is *not* a unit — it has\nnorm 3 — so it holds in a domain but not over an arbitrary `CommRing`. Only\nthe direction actually used is claimed.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.evenSubtree_no_oddPreimage","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.evenSubtree_no_oddPreimage","kind":"theorem","line":398,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L398-L410","doc":"**The obstruction.** If a node's cube-root evaluation is not a unit, then\n**no** node in its even-subtree admits an odd-preimage — the block is\npermanent, not local.\n\nThis is why the `k ≥ 1` odd branches of the backward tree can never chain a\nsecond odd step: each lands on a non-unit evaluation (norm `1 + 6k + 12k²`),\nand every even descendant inherits that. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.evenChain_isUnit_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.evenChain_isUnit_iff","kind":"theorem","line":386,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L386-L396","doc":"Iterating: unit-ness survives any number of even branch-inverses. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.evenPreimage_isUnit_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.evenPreimage_isUnit_iff","kind":"theorem","line":374,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L374-L384","doc":"**Unit-ness of the evaluation is invariant along an even chain.** The even\nbranch-inverse multiplies `P(w)` by the unit `w + 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.isUnit_eval_of_oddPreimage","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.isUnit_eval_of_oddPreimage","kind":"theorem","line":366,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L366-L372","doc":"**Only a unit evaluation can admit an odd-preimage.** Combining\n`oddPreimage_forces_eval_cubeRoot` (`P(w) = -w`) with the unit-ness of `-w`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.isUnit_one_add_cubeRoot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.isUnit_one_add_cubeRoot","kind":"theorem","line":361,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L361-L364","doc":"`w + 1` is a unit, with inverse `-w`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.isUnit_neg_cubeRoot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.isUnit_neg_cubeRoot","kind":"theorem","line":356,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L356-L359","doc":"`-w` is a unit, with inverse `-w²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.cubeRoot_pow_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.cubeRoot_pow_three","kind":"theorem","line":351,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L351-L354","doc":"`w³ = 1` for a primitive cube root. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.attractor_no_oddPreimage","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.attractor_no_oddPreimage","kind":"theorem","line":278,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L278-L289","doc":"**The attractor admits no odd-preimage.** `q⁻¹` evaluates to `w⁻¹ = w²` at a\nprimitive cube root, and `w² = -w` would force `1 = 0`. So no `q`-odd element\nmaps to the attractor under the odd branch — it is reached only by even\nsteps.\n\nThis is the structural reason odd-preimages are rare in the backward tree,\nand it is proved rather than observed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.oddPreimage_forces_eval_cubeRoot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.oddPreimage_forces_eval_cubeRoot","kind":"theorem","line":261,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L261-L276","doc":"**The cube-root criterion.** If `P` is the odd-branch image of `Q` —\n`[2]_q·P = [3]_q·Q + 1` — then at a primitive cube root `w` the `[3]_q` term\nvanishes and `P(w)` is pinned to `-w`, with `Q` eliminated entirely.\n\nSo `P(w) = -w` is **necessary** for `P` to admit an odd-preimage. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.cubeRoot_one_add_mul_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.cubeRoot_one_add_mul_neg","kind":"theorem","line":256,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L256-L259","doc":"`w + 1` is a **unit** whenever `w² + w + 1 = 0`, with inverse `-w`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.consecutive_odd_steps_exist","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.consecutive_odd_steps_exist","kind":"theorem","line":204,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L204-L224","doc":"**Two consecutive odd steps are possible.** Taking `A = -2` gives a `q`-odd\nelement whose odd-branch image is again `q`-odd — so the \"odd then even\"\nblock shape is *not* a theorem about the map, only about orbits where\n`A(-1) = -1`.\n\nConcretely over `ℤ[q]` this is `P = -2q - 3 ↦ -2q² - 3q - 2`, both of residue\n`-1`. Measured separately\n(`computations/qcollatz_block_structure_probe.py`): of 1420 such\nconsecutive-odd starts, **none** reached the attractor cycle — every one left\nthe domain, 93 % of them within two steps. So no `v = 0` block has been\nexhibited inside `D∞`, but none is excluded either: whether one can occur\nthere is **open**, and it is the precise obstacle to treating the block\ndecomposition as total. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.oddStep_next_residue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.oddStep_next_residue","kind":"theorem","line":189,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L189-L202","doc":"**Residue of the odd branch's output.** At the divisor `q = -1`, the output\n`-q + [3]_q·A` has residue `1 + A(-1)`. So what follows an odd step is\ndecided entirely by `A(-1)`:\n\n* `A(-1) = -1` → residue `0` → the next step is **even** (the block continues);\n* `A(-1) = -2` → residue `-1` → the next step is **odd** (a `v = 0` block);\n* anything else → the orbit **leaves the domain**.\n\nThis is the trichotomy that makes \"block\" well defined. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.oddStep_of_qOdd_form","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.oddStep_of_qOdd_form","kind":"theorem","line":178,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L178-L187","doc":"**The odd branch on a `q`-odd element.** With `P = -1 + [2]_q·A`,\n\n  [3]_q·P + 1 = [2]_q·(-q + [3]_q·A),\n\nso the branch's exact division is visible term by term and its output is\n`C(P) = -q + [3]_q·A`. A pure ring identity — no hypothesis on `q` or `A`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.reaches_cycle_of_block_descent_domainStable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.reaches_cycle_of_block_descent_domainStable","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L145-L166","doc":"**Convergence over `D∞` reduces to BLOCK descent.** The domain-stable\ncounterpart of `reaches_cycle_of_descent_domainStable`, with the weaker\nhypothesis. Forward-invariance is free, as in the per-step version, because\n`D∞` is closed under iteration.\n\nThis is the sharpest reduction of the `q`-Collatz convergence half currently\nin the corpus: the open content is now the existence of a measure that drops\n*somewhere ahead*, rather than at every step — which is the form the\ncontact-boundary condition `⟨v_[2]⟩ ≥ 2` actually takes. It supplies no such\nmeasure. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.domainStable_iterate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.domainStable_iterate","kind":"theorem","line":135,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L135-L143","doc":"**`D∞` is closed under iteration**, not merely under one step — the\niterated form of `QCollatz.domainStable_forward`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.block_descent_measure_pos_off_target","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.block_descent_measure_pos_off_target","kind":"theorem","line":119,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L119-L131","doc":"**Any `ℕ`-valued block-descent measure is still strictly positive off the\ntarget.** `μ (f^[n] x) < μ x` is impossible when `μ x = 0`, for every `n`. So\nthe `qou-4atn` refutation of `v_[2]` applies verbatim to block descent: the\nweaker hypothesis buys nothing for a measure vanishing on the `q`-odd part of\n`D∞`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.per_step_is_block_descent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.per_step_is_block_descent","kind":"theorem","line":104,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L104-L113","doc":"**Per-step descent is the `n = 1` case**, so block descent is a strictly\nweaker hypothesis than `reaches_of_descent_of_forward`'s and this reduction\nsubsumes it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.block_length_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.block_length_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":96,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L96-L102","doc":"A descending block has positive length — so the omitted `0 < n` hypothesis\nwould have been redundant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.reaches_of_block_descent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.CollatzBlockDescent.reaches_of_block_descent","kind":"theorem","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/collatz_block_descent.lean#L69-L94","doc":"**Block descent suffices for convergence.** If from every non-target point of\na cone some finite number of steps ahead the measure has strictly dropped —\nand the cone is closed under taking that many steps — then every point of the\ncone reaches the target.\n\nThe step count `n` may vary from point to point and is not bounded. Note it\nis automatically positive: `n = 0` would give `μ x < μ x`\n(`block_length_ne_zero`), so requiring `0 < n` as a hypothesis would be\nredundant and it is omitted. ","signatureChunk":0}]}